ea429c904ca49700fca369fe60d0237c48a91a76a09f6636835d92f9b416.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x+1)(2-y).$ What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/d5d7c89d619aa13d7fea8bc1260a08cb8678fb260e4431508c5f4c8c53e7ba5e.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/6cce64627f21f6cc17efc789614a0bd582ded827a0938f01c9b756e0cb461862.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/c24cc3ab0812bfae6834ff8c3a68668c3925f6e39e29a453ea6bc27fbc9f6c74.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6654aab1c6684aea77ce060515556c098d052c432e70a442ff799a3ef4cb0f6"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |"] ["text" "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |"] ["text" "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |"] ["text" "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |"] ["text" "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-121278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+2){y}^{3}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/975b596621dab7d455b09d75b1c8d2b9fd73e75cd2169ec40aadda48673f6883.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/9466a3fbf682ce967cda2294cfb61d5fa40b4f7c91686cfc8653cb4c1ffb4e97.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/b8e5807115c13aa842364a174058d1081bd84eba10dce13390da93832a1aeb54.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6654aab1c6684aea77ce060515556c098d052c432e70a442ff799a3ef4cb0f6"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/0ba31669fe6eb9d3eadd9423de52ea1042300fdc098cb3e9bd8081ed458c0d07.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/8a9becf0529b2896627d44fef11066d684c50479e62166ccc777a6250cd24033.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/97e1785d876935df14a9d7cea236f536c90b68fa1bad2bf8418569c80e56ba12.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f0614c91d93dc23135aad96e908562cfe1f6726f1c2b641f0ee55952deb4625b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f12e4490704485f3b05921fe9e25b21bc04aa389995ec2e4580593f63883e47c.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/97e1785d876935df14a9d7cea236f536c90b68fa1bad2bf8418569c80e56ba12.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(3-x){y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/e4d289aef282e076b062aab6d8f0f209396313e2c6403b2e27d1c0272ac90795.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/4cef3532a8e70098c030f2341800f06e9e54e4074126a0a97aecdf1f1c5185d0.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/c9ed172c5746b1b0afd2d9b7b3bbb21245115df164bfb7e34f4f530887376e28.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6654aab1c6684aea77ce060515556c098d052c432e70a442ff799a3ef4cb0f6"} {"question" "q-149550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748e4bc320dc2"} {"question" "q-149550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/1cadc33591f95b5b66f50794e329768364708bb7f9de1e06d579ff1833be11d4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/2f661772948f506a7fa8f0a205cf2e84b3a10dfc9bc35b695d572755ca35c917.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/7c1521cfad08f4c1cc4a699066ec5dc84fd635c2f1d39f71dca5a8ba86533887.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc934f768d8c34fe208e4058990.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f0547f4bf2530cbcc271e81ba501c3710b0359bb222d1e56014acac6675b515d.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/state.json" "file_sha256" "32477fdf5e6b33fd9ddf098c73640d0846e4f52944145be064b748